{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791594519,"nonce":"30scddfKSpLgPKyoi+2fRQNga1k=","title":"Top layer and anchored placement","message":"A layer above the app for what floats over it, and placement of that against an anchor. Popover, Tooltip, Select, Menu, Dialog and Toast in a Base UI-style adapter all sit on it.\n\nScope\n- A root-level layer: a dimmed backdrop, dismissal by an outside press and by Escape, focus returned to what held it, pass-through hits while something is closing, and a modeless mode for hover cards.\n- Placement: below, beside with flip and clamp to the viewport, at an edge, at a point; the chosen side exposed as a data-side attribute so a stylesheet can style it.\n- TypeScript only: absolute children of the root already hit first. Input.trapFocus is the only related primitive (src/native/input.ts). Nothing named top layer, popover or placement exists in src (checked).\n\nDepends on pointer-events and exit animations; pointer-events lands with the CSS paint router, exit animations need the motion issue (35f7bd0).\n\nReference, for structure: ashui's haxe/ashui/ui/TopLayer.hx, components/Floating.hx and ui/Dialog.hx. It has no arrow, offset or size middleware; a floating-ui core with a platform backed by HostElement.bounds() would give those.\n\nAcceptance: an anchored panel opens, flips at a viewport edge, closes on an outside press and on Escape, and returns focus; offscreen pixel and geometry tests for each side.","files":null}]}